; metacalc library reader for the TI-84 Plus SE. Reads a library in format MCL1
; (crates/mcpack/src/library/mod.rs) from the flash pages of the app for the screens: counts,
; labels, kinds, statements, hypotheses, symbol names, section titles, glyph codes and parse
; trees. The proof kernel (kernel/) checks the library; this code only reads it and trusts
; nothing it reads past the bounds it checks.
;
; Code runs from flash in bank A and is position dependent. Assembling this file alone places it
; at $4000, with -I at the repository root and at app/src so that "lib/ram.inc" and ram_map.inc
; are found; an including file defines LIB_EMBEDDED first and places the code itself. Either way LIB_FONT_FINGERPRINT (or
; UI_FONT_FINGERPRINT, already assembled from the font include) must be defined before this file.
;
; Memory. The app's RAM (page 81h) is in bank B, which is also where a library page has to be
; mapped to be read, so a library byte cannot be copied straight into an app buffer. lib_read
; copies in two hops through a bounce buffer on the spare RAM page (default page 3, mapped in
; bank C through port 5): flash to the bounce buffer with the library page in bank B, then the
; bounce buffer to the caller's buffer with the caller's bank B back.
;   LIB_BOUNCE, default $FE00 with the kernel's regions in place: the last 256 bytes of the
;     kernel's saved-symbols buffer, which is the end of the kernel's high region, and it must
;     lie inside that buffer. The kernel uses that buffer only during a kernel_verify call, so a
;     reader call between two kernel calls leaves the kernel alone; no other byte of the spare
;     page and no byte of page 80h is written.
;   LIB_STATE, default $989C, 725 bytes, in bank B after the scratch of probe/kerndriver.inc in
;     appBackUpScreen:
;     $00 scratch of lib_read, $10 facts from the header, $20 the record descriptor, $40
;     scratch, $60 a 32 byte window over the record being walked, $80 a cache of eight
;     directory entries, $A8 the symbol index: one 16-bit offset per symbol and one past the
;     last, 512 bytes, $2A8 the sections:
;       ls_have   $2A8  1  bit 0 titles, 1 glyph map, 2 trees, 4 shapes present; bit 3 the glyph
;                          map is for this build's font
;       ls_ti     $2A9  9  titles: offset, length, offset of the first item (3 bytes each)
;       ls_gl     $2B2  9  glyph map: the same
;       ls_tr     $2BB  9  trees: the same
;       ls_ti_n   $2C4  2  titles in the library
;       ls_gl_fp  $2C6  2  the fingerprint the library's glyph map carries
;       ls_tr_n   $2C8  2  syntax axioms
;       ls_ts     $2CA  3  scratch of the bounds check
;       ls_te     $2CD  3  scratch of the bounds check
;       ls_sh     $2D0  5  shapes: offset (3 bytes), length (2)
; The state, the caller's buffers and the caller's stack are all in the memory the caller sees
; at the call, bank B and bank C as mapped then.
;
; Stack. Between the port 5 write that maps the spare page and the one that restores the caller's
; page, lib_read holds no stack instruction at all: it keeps what it needs in the shadow
; registers, and the stack stays untouched in the caller's page. Everything else pushes and pops
; on the caller's stack, at most 16 bytes below the return address.
;
; Font. The glyph map is usable only when the fingerprint the library carries is
; LIB_FONT_FINGERPRINT, which the font include mcglyph z80 writes gives as UI_FONT_FINGERPRINT.
;
; Calling. Every routine returns its status in A, 0 meaning ok, with the zero flag set exactly
; when A is 0, and destroys all registers but IX and IY. lib_read puts values in the shadow registers only after it has turned interrupts
; off, since an interrupt handler uses them, and leaves them destroyed. A call with interrupts on
; turns them off for the two hops of a read, and on again before it returns; ports 5 and 7 and
; the interrupt state are the caller's again on every exit, whatever the status. A read that is
; refused maps nothing. The state, the saved ports and the interrupt flag are read back before
; the last copy, so a destination can never change them, and the public reads refuse any
; destination that touches the state block.
;
;   lib_read          in:  BC = byte count, 1 to 256; DE = destination, entirely in bank B
;                          ($8000 to $BFFF) and clear of the state block; A:HL = 24-bit library
;                          offset, A the high byte
;                     out: A = status, 4 until lib_open has returned 0. The bytes are in the
;                          destination only when it is 0.
;                          The library may end in any chunk: a read that crosses into the next
;                          16 KB chunk continues on the next lower flash page. The library is
;                          read through its length field, so the bytes of any later section
;                          are readable too.
;   lib_open          in:  A = flash page of chunk 0; DE = scratch of 256 bytes in bank B, used
;                          until the call returns, clear of the state block
;                     out: A = status, 2 for a scratch that is not allowed. Checks the magic, the length against the pages there
;                          are, the counts, the directory's place and alignment, and every
;                          symbol's typecode, and builds the symbol index. It does not check the
;                          CRC-32. A symbol name of 255 bytes is refused. The reader is closed
;                          until this returns 0.
;                          Sections: a library whose last four bytes are not "MCSX" has none and
;                          opens as before. Otherwise the trailer and the table of up to 28 rows
;                          are checked, and the offset and length of each known section kept in
;                          the state. Status 6: the version is not 1. Status 7: the table is
;                          damaged, meaning more than 28 rows, rows that do not fit between the
;                          start of the file and the trailer, a row off a multiple of four, a row
;                          that starts before the end of the directory or of the last record, ends
;                          past the table, overlaps another or repeats an id. Status 3: the last
;                          record cannot be walked or ends past the library, which is only looked
;                          at when there is a table.
;                          Status 8: a known section's index does not fit its row, or the trees
;                          list more than 383 syntax axioms, or the shapes section's count is not
;                          the trees', its offset table is longer than the section, its length
;                          does not fit 16 bits, or only one of shapes and trees is there. Unknown
;                          ids are skipped. A glyph map for another font, or for another number of
;                          symbols, does not fail the open.
;   lib_assertion_count   out: HL = assertions
;   lib_symbol_counts     out: B = constants, C = variables
;   lib_symbol_name   in:  A = symbol id; DE = buffer of 255 bytes
;                     out: B = name length, C = FFh for a constant, else the id of the
;                          variable's typecode; the name is in the buffer
;   lib_label         in:  HL = assertion number; DE = buffer of 255 bytes
;                     out: B = label length; the label is in the buffer
;   lib_record        in:  HL = assertion number
;                     out: fills the descriptor in the state block, which is the contract with
;                          later calls and with readers of the state:
;                            lr_dn        number the descriptor describes, FFFFh for none
;                            lr_drec      offset of the record, 3 bytes
;                            lr_dkind     0 axiom, 1 theorem
;                            lr_dllen     label length (the label is at lr_drec + 2)
;                            lr_dnhyps    hypothesis count
;                            lr_dhyps     offset of the first hypothesis, 3 bytes
;                            lr_dnpairs   disjoint pair count, 2 bytes
;                            lr_dpairs    offset of the first pair, 3 bytes
;                            lr_dconc     offset of the conclusion's first symbol, 3 bytes
;                            lr_dclen     conclusion length
;                          A call for the number the descriptor already holds returns at once.
;   lib_conclusion    in:  HL = assertion number; DE = buffer of 255 bytes
;                     out: B = length in symbols, one id per byte, in the buffer
;   lib_hypothesis    in:  HL = assertion number; B = hypothesis number; DE = buffer of 255 bytes
;                     out: B = length in symbols, C = FFh for an essential hypothesis, with its
;                          symbols in the buffer, else the variable of a float hypothesis,
;                          with B = 0. Consecutive numbers of one assertion cost one step each.
;   lib_sections       out: B = bits 0 titles, 1 glyph map, 2 trees, 4 shapes: which sections the library has
;   lib_title_count   out: HL = titles. 9 without a titles section.
;   lib_title         in:  HL = title number; DE = buffer of 255 bytes
;                     out: HL = the number of the first assertion under it, B = text length,
;                          C = depth, 1 to 4; the text, ASCII, is in the buffer
;   lib_title_for     in:  HL = assertion number
;                     out: HL = the number of the last title whose assertion number is at or
;                          before it, by binary search; 9 with HL = 0 when none is
;   lib_glyph_map     out: A = 0 when the map is for this build's font, 9 without a glyph map, 10
;                          for another font's or another symbol count; HL = the fingerprint the
;                          library's map carries, 0 without one. lib_glyph gives the same 9 and
;                          10, so a caller that sees 10 draws the symbols some other way.
;   lib_glyphs        out: A = status as lib_glyph_map gives it; E:HL = the section's offset in the
;                          library, BC = its length, FFFFh when it is 65,535 bytes or longer
;   lib_glyph         in:  A = symbol id; DE = buffer of 255 bytes
;                     out: C = spacing flags (bit 0 gap before, 1 gap after, 2 gap beside another
;                          variable, 3 no gap after), B = glyph count; the codes of the main
;                          display font are in the buffer
;   lib_syntax_count  out: HL = syntax axioms. 9 without a trees section.
;   lib_syntax        in:  HL = syntax index
;                     out: HL = the axiom's assertion number, B = its arity. The entry is returned
;                          as stored: the host decoder also refuses entries out of rising
;                          assertion order and an arity that is not the assertion's hypothesis
;                          count, and this does not check either.
;   lib_tree          in:  HL = assertion number; B = statement, the essential hypotheses in record
;                          order from 0, then the conclusion, which is also FFh; DE = buffer of
;                          255 bytes
;                     out: BC = the tree's length in bytes, E:HL = the offset of its first byte in
;                          the library, the first 255 bytes or all of them copied to the buffer;
;                          read the rest with lib_read at the offset. 9 for a statement the
;                          grammar read nothing from, and for a library without trees.
;   lib_shapes        out: A = status, 9 without the section; E:HL = the section's offset in the
;                          library, BC = its length, to copy with lib_read
;   lib_shape         in:  HL = syntax index; DE = buffer of 255 bytes
;                     out: B = part count, the part bytes in the buffer, 0 for a constant and k + 1
;                          for the child k. Status 9 without the section, 5 for an index past the
;                          table, 3 for bytes the format forbids: no parts, more than 255 bytes, an
;                          item outside the table's end or the section, an item across a 16 KB
;                          chunk, a child past the arity the trees list or one repeated or missing.
;                          The arity is read from the trees entry as stored, as lib_syntax returns
;                          it, without checking its assertion number. The host decoder also refuses
;                          a first offset off the table's end, a last offset off the section's end
;                          and an arity that is not the assertion's hypothesis count, and this does
;                          not check any of them: a gap after the table or bytes after the last
;                          item are never read.
; Statuses (LIB_ERR_*): 1 offset or length past the library, 2 a count or destination that is
; not allowed, 3 bytes the format forbids, 4 lib_open has not succeeded, 5 an assertion,
; hypothesis, symbol, title or syntax number out of range, 6 and 7 and 8 a damaged trailer, table
; or section index at open, 9 nothing there (no such section, or no tree for a statement), 10 a glyph
; map for another font. An accessor checks, in this order, that the reader is
; open (4), that its buffer is 255 bytes in bank B clear of the state block (2, whatever the
; string's length), that the section is there (9) and its glyph map usable (10), and its numbers
; (5). The section routines use no shadow register and no port: every library byte comes through
; lr_read, so the interrupt rules above hold for them as they do for lib_read. A lib_record that
; fails for any reason leaves lr_dn at FFFFh, describing no assertion, and so does lib_open.
; A title, glyph entry or tree item that crosses a 16 KB chunk is refused with status 3 when it is
; read, as the host decoder refuses it for the whole library. Strings are at most 255 symbols by
; the format.

#ifndef LIB_EMBEDDED
.org $4000
#endif

#include "lib/ram.inc"
#include "lib/read.inc"
#include "lib/open.inc"
#include "lib/symbol.inc"
#include "lib/window.inc"
#include "lib/record.inc"
#include "lib/sections.inc"
#include "lib/secshared.inc"
#include "lib/titles.inc"
#include "lib/glyphs.inc"
#include "lib/trees.inc"
#include "lib/shapes.inc"
